    "created_at": "2018-09-19T19:13:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-09-19T19:13:40Z",
    "body": "Okay, then the first paragraph of my previous post goes away.\r\n\r\nThat bug thread is a lot of reading. I did search it for the six status terms (three defined in the Standard and three used in the wiki) and found five occurrences \"ratified\" that discussed whether the definition in the Standard was okay. Nothing else about those definitions.\r\n\r\nIn the wiki page section heading [Proposals That Don't Meet the Requirements For a Registration](https://wiki.whatwg.org/wiki/MetaExtensions#Proposals_That_Don.27t_Meet_the_Requirements_For_a_Registration), the word \"Requirements\" links back to the [Other metadata names](http://www.whatwg.org/specs/web-apps/current-work/multipage/semantics.html#other-metadata-names) section of the Standard, where there is no specification of registration requirements.\r\n\r\nAre there any records of anything ever being ratified at all, or any way of legitimately accepting some of the proposals? Do all the proposals, including \"robots\", have to remain as proposals forever?\r\n\r\nIf so, perhaps you could replace the three definitions in the Standard with something like:\r\n\r\n- Proposal. A name that has been proposed and not rejected.\r\n- Incomplete proposal. A name proposed without sufficient information.\r\n- Unendorsed. A name proposed that does not satisfy __________________________.\r\n\r\nWhile it would be better if there were some way of indicating that some names are ratified, it seems to me that the most important thing is that the Standard and the wiki page it cites don't contradict each other.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
